---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can I check whether my shared IP has problems?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes: run the blacklist checker and IP reputation scan on the IP your ESP sends from; repeated listings point at pool trouble worth escalating to the provider."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does a dedicated IP guarantee better deliverability?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "No. It guarantees isolated reputation. Deliverability still depends on authentication, list quality, and engagement."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How long does IP warm-up take?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Typically two to six weeks depending on daily volume and engagement quality; the schedule matters more than the speed."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can I have both shared and dedicated?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes, and high-volume senders often do: dedicated for bulk marketing, shared or separate dedicated IP for transactional mail."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Dedicated vs Shared IP for Email Sending | PromptDNS"
  description="Dedicated vs shared IP for email: reputation isolation, warm-up requirements, cost, and how to decide which your sending volume actually needs."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Dedicated vs Shared IP for Email Sending</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">Dedicated vs Shared IP for Email Sending</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A shared IP means your mail leaves alongside other senders' traffic; a dedicated IP is yours alone. The choice shapes who controls your sender reputation, how fast you can ramp, and what happens when someone else misbehaves.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The trade-off, plainly</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60"><table class="pdns-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Shared IP</th><th>Dedicated IP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Reputation</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Shared with the pool: neighbors affect you</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Yours alone: total control</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Warm-up</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Instant (pool is pre-warmed)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">2-6 weeks of gradual ramp</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Volume needed</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Any</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Only makes sense above ~50k emails/month</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Cost</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Included with most ESPs</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Add-on fee or your own server</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">How to decide</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-ink">Under ~50k/month or irregular sending:</strong> shared IP. A cold dedicated IP with low volume looks worse than a warm shared pool.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">High, consistent volume:</strong> dedicated, so one bad neighbor can never touch you and reputation is fully yours to build.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Mixed mail (transactional + marketing):</strong> dedicated IPs for each stream, so marketing problems never delay receipts and password resets.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">If you go dedicated: warm-up discipline</h2>
      <p>A fresh IP has zero reputation. Start at a few hundred messages a day, roughly double every two to three days while engagement stays healthy, and keep authentication (<a href="/spf-record/">SPF</a>, <a href="/dkim/">DKIM</a>, <a href="/dmarc/">DMARC</a>) airtight throughout. Rushing warm-up is the fastest route to the spam folder.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I check whether my shared IP has problems?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: run the blacklist checker and IP reputation scan on the IP your ESP sends from; repeated listings point at pool trouble worth escalating to the provider.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does a dedicated IP guarantee better deliverability?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No. It guarantees isolated reputation. Deliverability still depends on authentication, list quality, and engagement.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How long does IP warm-up take?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Typically two to six weeks depending on daily volume and engagement quality; the schedule matters more than the speed.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I have both shared and dedicated?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, and high-volume senders often do: dedicated for bulk marketing, shared or separate dedicated IP for transactional mail.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/why-emails-go-to-spam/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Why emails go to spam →</a></li><li><a href="/blacklist-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Blacklist checker →</a></li><li><a href="/email-deliverability/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Email deliverability hub →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
